   "source": [
    "# Report: zero-shot vs. few-shot prompting\n",
    "\n",
    "**Setup.** `gpt-3.5-turbo`, `temperature=0`. Every variant was tested on the same questions: 5 for movies, and 10 labelled examples each for sentiment and tickets. Numbers are from a single run, so a 1-point difference is not meaningful. A 2+ point difference, or a different *kind* of answer, is.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Results\n",
    "\n",
    "| Experiment | Variant | Result |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Movie facts | zero-shot | Correct facts, but **0/5** answers in the format. It also wrote a whole paragraph about *Parasite*. |\n",
    "| | few-shot (2 examples) | **3/5** in format. It correctly refused both trap questions, in free text. |\n",
    "| | few-shot + \"unknown\" example | 4/5 \"in format\", but it **hallucinated both traps** (see below). |\n",
    "| | instruction + few-shot + \"unknown\" example | **5/5** in format, and both traps answered with `unknown`. |\n",
    "| Sentiment (4 labels) | zero-shot / balanced shots / all-Positive shots | **9/10 for all three.** Identical answers. |\n",
    "| Ticket routing (invented codes) | codes only | 8/10 |\n",
    "| | written instructions | **10/10** |\n",
    "| | shots in system prompt | 9/10, but answered `Code: PAY-7` once (format leak) |\n",
    "| | shots as user/assistant turns | 8/10 |\n",
    "| | instructions + shots as turns | **10/10** |\n",
    "\n",
    "## What didn't work\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **The format examples caused hallucinations.** When the examples (the shots) included one where the right answer was \"unknown\", the model stopped refusing the trap questions. It filled in the template instead:\n",
    "   - It gave a real director for a film that doesn't exist. The name was **\"Sasa Gedeon\"** on the first run and **\"Slobodan Šijan\"** on the second, even at temperature 0.\n",
    "   - It rewrote the false premise as *\"Midnight Orchard (original title: The Artist), Michel Hazanavicius\"*. It merged the fake title with a real Oscar winner, and the year is wrong too: *The Artist* won at the 2012 ceremony.\n",
    "\n",
    "   Without that example, the model refused both traps correctly. My reading is that the extra example strengthened the pull towards \"always produce Title/Year/Director\" more than it taught \"say unknown\". **My automatic format check counted these hallucinations as successes**, so format compliance is not correctness.\n",
    "2. **The same prompt also dropped a field.** The *Titanic* answer lost its `Director:` line in both runs.\n",
    "3. **One example per class can't teach a rule.** Neither shots-only variant learned that \"threatens a lawyer\" overrides the topic: *\"you double-charged me, my lawyer will contact you\"* was routed to `PAY-7`, not `ESC-9`. \"Download the invoice for my subscription\" went to `ACC-1`. Five examples don't tell the model which features matter.\n",
    "4. **Examples pasted into the system prompt leak their layout.** I wrote the examples as `...\\nCode: PAY-7`, and the model copied the `Code:` prefix once. The role-based examples didn't have this problem.\n",
    "5. **A fix can make answers less helpful.** The combined prompt answered the \"Midnight Orchard\" question with the exact `Note:` text from the example (\"I can't find a film with this title\"). The zero-shot model was more useful: it also gave the real winner, *The King's Speech*.\n",
    "\n",
    "## What surprised me\n",
    "\n",
    "- **For sentiment, the examples made no difference.** Even four examples that were all `Positive` didn't bias the model. Sentiment is a task gpt-3.5 already knows, so examples add little.\n",
    "- The one \"mistake\" (*\"Five stars for the delivery guy, zero for the product\"* → `Mixed`) is arguably my label's fault, not the model's. **Test-set labels need care too.**\n",
    "\n",
    "## What I learned\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Examples teach format; instructions teach rules.** Examples were the fastest way to get a consistent output shape. Business rules, such as an override or what to do when information is missing, worked reliably only when written out. **Combining both gave the best results in both experiments that needed it.**\n",
    "- **Examples are most useful when the task is new to the model** (invented codes, a custom format), and least useful for tasks it already knows (sentiment).\n",
    "- **Every example you add changes behaviour in ways you didn't intend.** Always test the trap questions again after adding one.\n",
    "- **Measure, don't eyeball.** Reading one answer per prompt, as we did in class, would have hidden almost everything in this report. A 10-row labelled test set was enough to rank the prompts.\n",
    "- **Prefer user/assistant turns over pasting examples into the system prompt.** It's cleaner and doesn't leak the example layout. Here it didn't improve accuracy on its own.\n"
   ]
  }
